WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:08.050
My name is clay Moffat I'm a filmmaker
in San Tan Valley Arizona. In my

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:12.130
professional film career I guess I've
made probably five or six short films

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:17.500
and currently working on my third
feature film right now. As director on

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.570
average I shoot a feature film in about
three or four days. I do all the writing

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:29.320
directing producing and editing myself
I'm able to support my family and keep

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:35.289
throwing money and income into films by
having a full-time job I actually manage

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:42.760
a pizza restaurant and I work 55 hours
every single week in addition to seeing

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.840
my wife raising my kids and living that
lifestyle and also producing films so

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:57.129
there's only 24 hours in a day and I
stretch it thin the secret is caffeine I

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:03.010
would say the secret and a lot of people
tell me this too is the secret is

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.210
managing your time and I think that I'm
able to do that so well because I've

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.820
been a restaurant manager for 12 years
so I understand the scheduling and the

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.840
aspect that goes with that and I just
put that into filmmaking and my daily

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:20.439
life my favorite role in the whole
process is definitely post-production

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:25.149
editing I really like doing the visual
effects work but I also like cutting the

00:01:25.149 --> 00:01:30.460
film together I think it's it's funny
how different things can turn out just

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.329
with simple cutting and editing so
that's definitely I think where the

00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:37.420
magic happens and whether or not whether
or not your movies gonna make it or

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.600
break it
my least favorite part of the whole

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:47.890
process is the before I like writing
don't get me wrong but I think from the

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:53.350
coming up with the idea and then writing
it and then before you shoot like the

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:58.600
casting and the planning and finding
locations I want to give that to someone

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.909
else I'm not much of a planner and maybe
that's why my process works the way it

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.780
does for me is because I'm much more
let's just go do it I want to make a

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.310
movie I don't care what that location
looks like as long as we can do a shot

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:17.690
in it my process for casting
and following through with the cast I

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:23.320
posted audition notices on my overnight
pictures page my personal Facebook page

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:29.330
some people that I know would then share
it in like acting pages on Facebook that

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.400
I'm not a part of stuff like that just
get get out there as much as many eyes

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.050
as can see it so that we have the
biggest pool coming back to us so I got

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:45.350
I think the final tally was like a
hundred and twenty eight emails from

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:50.900
people that wanted to be a part of this
movie and I sent them back sides for

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.250
characters that I felt they looked like
so some people got two or three sides

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.600
some only got one some of them got the
majority of characters sides and they

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.190
sent back a video and I basically weed
out that way and from there we actually

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:09.080
had live auditions but the number had
been cut in half or less by that point

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:16.100
once the cast has been picked we do
table reads I kind of get on everybody

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.630
for weeks and weeks leading up to the
shoot and then I think my philosophy on

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:25.750
shooting so quick works because they
don't have time to get out of character

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:31.430
they stay in character the whole shoot
they're like boom ready to go the whole

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:37.010
three or four days when I post the
casting calls I actually include that

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.270
it's going to be only a three or four
days shoot you're gonna work 12 plus

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:46.910
hour days because that's what my work
schedule allows me to get off so I you

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:51.800
know I know some people the majority of
people will shoot a couple of days take

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:57.290
days off whatever take you know a month
long four weeks whatever it is but my

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:03.160
life and my schedule doesn't allow that
right now so it's

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:11.760
tough when we're shooting one of my
movies the way that it works is I

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.560
usually yell action and I let the actors
go and if they mess up they just keep

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.150
going and we'll usually get two or three
takes I try to make sure one of them is

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:26.340
a close-up so sometimes I might not be
getting exactly what I want and other

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.320
directors might want to shoot eight or
nine takes but I think for me as long as

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.380
you're getting one of everything that
you're happy with you're gonna be okay

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:43.470
because at the end of the day only one
of those takes is going in the movie now

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:48.000
you can cut it up and mix it up however
you want but really only one visual is

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.880
gonna go on that frame I think a lot of
people overcompensate for that because

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.140
they get eight or nine but it's like
dude you're only gonna put one of those

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.520
in your movie and if you're happy with
what you're seeing then there's no point

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.229
in going on especially when you're on
such a tight schedule in the past some

00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:11.490
of my films I I see what they're gonna
look like and then they come out and I'm

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.400
happy with them but I'm not like that's
how I envisioned that to look

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.360
particularly with silver woods now
silver woods was shot in a day and a

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:28.440
half so that leaves a little room for
error like if you mess up it you pretty

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.260
much what we have to go on that's tough
but I would say it it's different with

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.720
silver woods and some of my other films
because we didn't plan out how things

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.220
were gonna look we had a story we had a
script we had a cast we had a location

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.030
but we didn't really get to do scouting
we didn't really get to set up what

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:52.680
things were gonna look like and it was
just kind of will play a by ear when we

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.450
get there and set up with pocket man and
cargo boy it was a little bit different

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:01.770
we did a little bit more planning we got
to see pictures of locations ahead of

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.860
time so we got to kind of plan out what
things were gonna look like but I would

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.360
say the biggest difference between Pocky
man and cargo boy is I wrote POC man in

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.460
cargo boy when I was 13 I've already
made Pocky man and cargo boy twice this

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.360
is the third time I'm making this
feature it's the time that I'm making it

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:22.680
the way I want it to look so I've known
what this movie and this whole series is

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.509
going to look like for a decade and to
be able to get the budget the actors the

00:06:27.509 --> 00:06:34.680
crew the location the camera to be able
to do what I want to do was awesome I

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:41.400
have found myself upset with the footage
I have not done reshoots I've had to

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:46.469
work around it sometimes we don't get
access to the locations that we were at

00:06:46.469 --> 00:06:53.729
because they were rented I've gone in
the way of finding places through like

00:06:53.729 --> 00:07:00.029
Airbnb and the owners will let me use
the house or the the cabin whatever it

00:07:00.029 --> 00:07:05.939
might be but five months down the road
when I'm editing and I'm not happy with

00:07:05.939 --> 00:07:09.719
something I might not have access to
that place again for example when we

00:07:09.719 --> 00:07:13.919
shot the October flowers the owner
actually sold the house a week after we

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:17.699
wrapped so I don't know that the new
owner would even let us back in there

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:21.870
and there's no guarantee that any of the
furniture would still be in there I'm

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.960
assuming they took all their stuff with
them so reshoots were kind of out of the

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.539
question I just kind of have to find
tricks through edit and fix it or cut

00:07:30.539 --> 00:07:35.789
the scene entirely which I don't do very
often I usually figure out how to kind

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:39.270
of make it all work but there's
definitely times where I'm like oh we

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:46.050
should have done this why did we do that
my approach to how I handle actors and

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:50.069
crew on set is probably not very ideal
compared to how other directors might do

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:55.310
things my philosophy is always have fun
and make sure everyone is comfortable

00:07:55.310 --> 00:08:01.199
because I believe that if they like what
they're doing and the environment is fun

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:04.860
they're going to give a better
performance have a better time and be

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.680
more understanding if you might fall an
hour behind schedule but it might not

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:16.110
work for other people if you're the
director and you're walking around and

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:21.439
you're this mean old guy that especially
the kids might be scared of or

00:08:21.439 --> 00:08:26.699
intimidated by I think you're gonna just
the environment is gonna be different I

00:08:26.699 --> 00:08:29.420
mean if you're gonna be working for 14
plus

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:33.920
hours you gotta like who you're with
it's no different than any other job I

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.540
would never go to a place where I got to
work a full day and my boss is just this

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:43.400
person that I'm scared to approach so I
directed Paki man and cargo boy in a

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:52.760
robe and it was fun everybody had a fun
time and it was light-hearted and it's

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.160
different I know but it works I'm
actually self-taught in filmmaking

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:04.400
I grew up making movies in my parents
house that was my location that I had

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:12.530
access to so I wrote stories around that
idea and I think as I've grown I've been

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:18.770
able to get outside the box a little bit
I've been able to get locations go and

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:26.540
get forests and mountains and whatever I
wanted that my story was needing but I

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:33.320
think that that idea of being limited
early on has helped me out now because

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:38.230
if I find something that I'm not sure
how to do or don't have the money for it

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:44.630
doesn't scare me I'm able to well look I
made 50 short films in my parents house

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:50.600
growing up I can figure this out the
idea for paquimé and Kargil boy was

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.590
written when I was 13 living at home
with my parents but I was able to take

00:09:54.590 --> 00:10:00.260
that idea as an adult and a professional
filmmaker and turn it into something so

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:05.090
much bigger but still following the same
idea I eventually did go to film school

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:09.230
I went to Collins College and learned
avid and got to play with some really

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:14.450
expensive cameras and that was nice and
it taught me networking but I think it's

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:21.710
important to understand that a filmmaker
can be by the books a filmmaker can be

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:29.360
someone who has all the experience and
knows every aspect of filmmaking and a

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:32.810
filmmaker can also be someone who's a
beginner that just wants to learn their

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:36.350
way because that's kind of how I started
I used to tell my friends one day I'm

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.200
gonna make big movies and people are
gonna watch them

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:45.650
they laughed you know I have always had
support but I don't think it's worth

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:49.490
adducts to go the way that I went but
that doesn't mean that you can't find

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:54.050
your own way you don't have to do what
everyone else is doing and conform I got

00:10:54.050 --> 00:11:00.470
to go to film school I got to be a PA on
a big shoot you can do whatever you want

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:06.680
and you don't need a lot of money I'm
able to make all my projects for really

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:14.540
under 22 even sometimes ten thousand
dollars so it's not a ton of money being

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:22.070
thrown out there all at once it's
possible to make quality work with a

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:27.470
shoestring budget there's a difference
in being ambitious and being there's no

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.490
way in heck you're going to be able to
pull that off and you have to be clear

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.870
on that when you go in because if you go
into something knowing that you can't

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:40.760
pull it off you're gonna fail from the
start but I think that everybody should

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:48.080
probably take away the idea that just
because you're limited doesn't mean you

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.160
can't make something big

